<<data, include=FALSE>>=
### Loading data from "copd_redux.RData" file in the working directory:
load("./copd_redux.RData")
@

<<missingness>>=
n_obs <- nrow(copd) # Number of participants
n_vars <- ncol(copd) # Number of variables
nans <- 100*colMeans(is.na(copd)) # Percentage of missing data by variable
min_nan <- sprintf("%.2g", min(nans)) # MIRAR # Lower percentage
max_nan <- sprintf("%.2g", max(nans)) # Upper percentage

# Vairables with less than 5% of data missing (percentage)
less_5 <- sprintf("%.2g", 100*length(nans[nans<5])/n_vars) 

# Percentage of complete cases
comp_cases <- sprintf("%.2g", 100*mean(complete.cases(copd)))

# Percentage of missing data
miss_total <-  sprintf("%.2g", 100*mean(is.na(copd)))

# Total cells observed (variables*observations)
total_cells = prettyNum(n_obs*n_vars, big.mark = ",", decimal.mark = ".")
@

“The subset used here included \Sexpr{n_obs} participants and \Sexpr{n_vars} variables. The range of missing data in the different variables went from \Sexpr{min_nan}\% to \Sexpr{max_nan}\%, with \Sexpr{less_5}\% of the variables having less than 5\% of the data missing. Only \Sexpr{comp_cases}\% of the participants had complete data on all \Sexpr{n_vars} variables. Overall, \Sexpr{miss_total}\% of the information was missing; that is, \Sexpr{miss_total}\% of the total of \Sexpr{total_cells} cells (\Sexpr{n_vars} × \Sexpr{n_obs} = \Sexpr{total_cells}) had missing data.”

\subsection{Multiple imputation}

Apply multiple imputation to copd data using 100 imputations. Explain in a reasoned manner how you decided to set the values of the arguments \texttt{method} and \texttt{predictorMatrix} when using the \texttt{mice()} function. Show your code.

<<imputation_ini>>=

# Display of default imputation methods for each variable
ini <- mice(copd, maxit=0)
ini
@

We can see that the mice package suggest imputing initialy with de mean for continuous variables and with a logistic regression for binary ones. Further research on the dataset has shown that the variables have no deterministic relation. So we do not change the imputation methods. We have a variable (\texttt{id}) that don't have any role in the imputation process, so we change \texttt{predictorMatrix} and \texttt{methods} acording to that.

<<imputation_parameters>>=
# Change in parameters
meth <- ini$method
pred <- ini$predictorMatrix

meth["id"] <- "" # do not impute "id"
pred["id", ] <- 0 # we don't want "id" to be imputed
pred[, "id"] <- 0 # we don't want "id" to be predictor
@

<<imputation>>=
# Imputation of 100 datasets
M <- 100 # Number of imputations for each missing value
imp <- mice(data = copd, 
            m = M, 
            method = meth, 
            predictorMatrix = pred,
            maxit = 20,
            printFlag = FALSE,
            seed = 666)
# imp$imp (output llarg)
#summary(imp)
@


\subsection{Integration of multiple imputation in cluster analysis}

Reproduce the original analysis in the paper using the miclust package to integrate multiple
imputation in the cluster analysis. Apply the proposed framework using the same setting
than in the original analysis. To do that, follow these steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Read the help of the \texttt{getdata()} function in the \texttt{miclust} package. Then, accordingly, 
  create a \textit{list} of \textit{data.frames} and pass it to the \texttt{getdata()} function. Give a detailed
  explanation of the data processing you did to get that \textit{list}. Show your code.
  
<<data_prep>>=
# ?miclust::getdata
# getdata() requires the data to be a list or data.frame where the first element is the raw data:
  
  #1- prepare raw data (all numeric and without id) 
copd_raw <- copd
copd_raw$id <- NULL
num_cols <- sapply(copd_raw, function(x) is.numeric(x) || is.integer(x) || is.logical(x))
copd_raw <- copd_raw[, num_cols]

  #2- prepare imputated data
imp_list <- complete(imp, action = "all") # extracts the completed data from a mids object
imp_list_clean <- lapply(imp_list, function(df) {
  df$id <- NULL
  df <- df[, num_cols]
  return(df)
})

  #3- create list where the first element is the raw data
my_cluster_list <- c(list(copd_raw), imp_list_clean)

# prepare the data for miclust
imp_midata <- miclust::getdata(data = my_cluster_list) 

# now we check
class(imp_midata)
length(imp_midata$impdata) 
@

The \texttt{getdata()} function needs a \texttt{data.frames} list where the first element contains the original data (with missing values) and the remaining ones correspond to multiple imputed datasets. That is why we created \texttt{my\_cluster\_list}, the list of data frames that we pass to \texttt{getdata()}. The imputed data sets were extracted from the \texttt{mids}
object, and the same processing was applied to the original data and to each imputed data set, so that all of them have the same variables in the same order.
Since k-means groups the subjects using Euclidean distances, we removed the variables that are neither numeric nor logical. We also removed the \texttt{id} variable, because it is just an identifier and does not contain any valuable clinical information. For the same reason, the \texttt{id} was not included as a predictor in the imputation.
  
  \item Use the \texttt{miclust()} function to perform the integration of missing data in cluster
  analysis using the same settings as in the original analysis. Show your code and
  explain how and why you set all arguments in the \texttt{miclust()} function.

<<cluster_analysis>>=
miclust_1 <- miclust(data = imp_midata, 
                     method = "kmeans", 
                     search = "backward",
                     ks = 2:5,
                     usedimp = 1:100,
                     seed = 666)

#summary(miclust_1)
miclust_1$kfin # optimal k (number of clusters)
@

We need to perform a variable selection because adding variables can degrade the classification when the number of subjects is small compared to the numer of variables. As mentioned in the article, it is good practice that the number of subjects are at least 10 times the number of variables.
In our case, the ratio is $\Sexpr{n_obs} / \Sexpr{n_vars - 1} < 10$, so a reduction of the number of variables is needed.

As in the original analysis, we use the backward search with CritCF criterion. It starts with all the variables and, at each step, removes the variable that when excluded gives the highest CritCF. This criterion allows to rank partitions with different numbers of clusters and different numbers of variables.
Finally, we define \texttt{k = 2:5} where the maximum value was fixed a priori, the same way as the article. Since the backward seach is repeated for each $k$ in each imputed data set, limiting the number of clusters reduces the computational cost and also keeps the resulting groups interpretable.


\end{enumerate}

\section{Results}

Using \texttt{plot()} and \texttt{summary()} methods in the \texttt{miclust} 
package, provide plots and tables for the results similar than those shown in 
the original paper. Also, show a barplot for the percentage of times each 
variable is selected among the imputed data sets, in decreasing order. Explain 
how you set all arguments in all functions and methods you use. Show your code.

<<fig_critcf, fig.pos='H', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=8, out.width='0.9\\textwidth', fig.cap="\\label{fig:critcf}Boxplot of CritCF by number of clusters ($k$) over the 100 imputed data sets. Each box is based on 100 values; higher CritCF is preferred.">>=

# Distribution of the CritCF criterion over the imputed data sets, one box per
# explored number of clusters (ks = 2:5);
plot(miclust_1, metric = "critcf", col.critcf = "darkmagenta")
@

<<fig_varsel, fig.pos='H', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=6, out.width='0.9\\textwidth', fig.cap=paste0("\\label{fig:varsel}Variables that remained in the final set of variables in at least one of the ", length(miclust_1$usedimp), " imputed data sets after the backward selection algorithm for $k=", miclust_1$kfin, "$, by percentage of appearance. Horizontal lines show the first and third quartiles, median and mean of the number of selected variables.")>>=

# Final number of clusters selected by miclust
kfin <- miclust_1$kfin

# Percentage of appearance of each variable (metric = "varsel") for k = kfin
plot(miclust_1, k = kfin, metric = "varsel", col.varsel = "skyblue")
@


<<summaries>>=
# Set quantilevars = 0.5 so the numbers of final variables equals the median 
# number of variables selected across the imputed data sets.
summary_k <- summary(miclust_1, quantilevars = 0.5)

summary_kp1 <- summary(miclust_1, k = kfin + 1, quantilevars = 0.5)
@

<<table_alloc, results='asis'>>=
# Distribution of the probability of assignment to each cluster, for k = kfin
a1 <- as.matrix(summary_k$allocationprobabilities)

# Same distribution, for k = kfin + 1
a2 <- as.matrix(summary_kp1$allocationprobabilities) 

# Numeric part, an empty row before each k block, followed by the rows of 
# the corresponding summary
vals <- rbind(rep(NA, 5), a1, rep(NA, 5), a2) 

# Text part, one label per row.
labels <- c(paste0("$k=", kfin, "$"),
            paste0("Cluster ", seq_len(nrow(a1))),
            paste0("$k=", kfin + 1, "$"),
            paste0("Cluster ", seq_len(nrow(a2)))) 

# Combine text part, first column, and numeric values into one data.frame
tab_alloc <- data.frame(labels, vals) 


# Create the xtable object. 'digits' has 7 elements: the 1st is for the 
#automatic row names (not printed), the 2nd for the text labels (no decimals
# apply), and the last 5 for the numeric columns (2 decimals each).
# The caption and the label are defined here.
alloc <- xtable(tab_alloc,
                digits = c(0, 0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2),
                align = c("l", "l", "c", "c", "c", "c", "c"),
                caption = paste0("Distribution of the frequency of assignment
                          (proportion of the ", length(miclust_1$usedimp),
                          " imputed data sets) to each cluster, for $k=", 
                          kfin, "$ and $k=", kfin + 1, "$."),
                label = "tab:alloc")

# Column names, centered and in bold, the first one is left blank
colnames(alloc) <- c("","\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Minimum}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf First quartile}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Median}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Third quartile}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Maximum}") 

# Print the table un LaTeX
print(alloc,
      table.placement = "ht",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = FALSE, # Row names are not printed, labels are in column 1
      include.colnames = TRUE,
      floating = TRUE,
      sanitize.text.function = force, 
      booktabs = TRUE) 
@

<<table_description, results='asis'>>=
vars <- summary_k$selectedvariables # El nom de les variables finals seleccionades, la primera columna de la taula
summary_cluster <- summary_k$summarybycluster # És la part del summary on es detallen les propietats dels clusters

# % of missing values of each variable (raw data)
miss <- summary_cluster$`%miss.` # agafem només la columna del % de valors perduts, la segona columna de la taula.

# Imputed data, mean of each variable by cluster (one column per cluster)
mean_imputed <- as.matrix(summary_cluster[, grep("^mean", names(summary_cluster))]) # Matriu la qual és (numero de variables seleccionades) x (numero k final), s'agafa només les columnes que comencen per "mean" i fa una matriu amb cada columna, una per cada cluster. Aixo es la part de cluster d'imputed data

# Majority-vote cluster of each participant
cl <- summary_k$cluster # vector amb 342 números (en aquest cap 1 o 2, ja que són el numero de clusters finals que tenim), el cluster de cada participant. Per exemple si el particicpant 1 és del cluster 2, c1[1] = 1

raw_means <- matrix(NA, nrow = length(vars), ncol = kfin) # Creem una matriu buida a on el numero de files siguin cada una de les variables seleccionades i el numero de columnes el numero de clusters que tinguem.

F_raw <- vector(length = length(vars)) # Crem un vector buit per anar guardant els valors de F value.

for (i in 1:length(vars)) {
      x <- copd_raw[[vars[i]]] # Afagem tots els valors dels participants de les variables significatives i ho gardem en un vector x.
      fit <- lm(x ~ factor(cl)) # ajustem a un model lineal, explicar x a partir del cluster a partir d'un model lineal.
      F_raw[i] <- anova(fit)$`F value`[1] # anova() és un data.frame amb una columna F value, la qual la primera fila és el valor que busquem i l'altre és NA (F value dels residus) i com que no ens interessa només agfem el primer valor [1].
  for (j in 1:kfin){
    raw_means[i,j] <- mean(x[cl == j], na.rm = TRUE) # Posem la mitjana de cada cluster en cada una de les variables significatives.
  }
}

# Ara fem el f value per la part imputada, la diferencia amb la part "crua" és que en lloc d'un sol conjunt de dades, hi ha en aquest cas 100 (una per cada imputació feta), cadascun amb els seus clusters. Es claulca una F per cada un i se'n fa la mitjana.

vector_clusters <- summary_k$clustervectors

n_imp <- ncol(vector_clusters)

F_imputed <- vector(length = length(vars))

for (i in 1:length(vars)){
  f_n_imp <- vector(length = n_imp)
  for (m in 1:n_imp){
    y <- imp_midata$impdata[[m]][[vars[i]]] # Valor de la imputació m en la variable i.
    fit <- lm(y ~ factor(vector_clusters[, m])) # Agafem els valors de cluster que te cada individu en la imputació m. posem factor per assegurar-nos que R interperta els valors com etiquetes i no com a quantitats.
    f_n_imp[m] <- anova(fit)$`F value`[1]
  }
  F_imputed[i] <- mean(f_n_imp)
}

# Cluster sizes, raw data (majority vote) and median over the imputed data sets
n_raw     <- as.vector(table(factor(cl, levels = seq_len(kfin)))) # Només hi ha un unic cluster, aixi que la mida és exacte.
n_imp_med <- summary_k$clusterssize[, "50%"]  # En cada imputació alguns participants poden canviar de cluster, de manera que cada imputació dona mides dizerents, i per posar un sol numero a la capçalera com ho fa l'article agafem la mediana.


# Variable labels with "_" escaped for LaTeX
labels <- gsub("_", "\\\\_", vars)

tab_desc <- data.frame(labels, miss, raw_means, F_raw, mean_imputed, F_imputed)

range_txt <- paste0("cluster ", seq_len(kfin), ": ",
                    summary_k$clusterssize[, "min."], "--",
                    summary_k$clusterssize[, "max."], collapse = "; ")

desc <- xtable(tab_desc,
               digits = c(0, 0, 1, rep(2, kfin), 2, rep(2, kfin), 2),
               align  = c("l", "l", rep("c", 2 * kfin + 3)),
               caption = paste0("Mean values of the ", length(vars),
                                " selected variables by cluster ($k=", kfin, "$). ",
                                "Raw data: majority-vote cluster assignment, ",
                                "missing values excluded. ",
                                "Imputed data: means over the ", n_imp,
                                " imputed data sets. ",
                                "$n$: number of participants per cluster (raw data: ",
                                "majority-vote assignment, imputed data: median over ",
                                "the imputed data sets, with minimum--maximum ",
                                range_txt, "). ",
                                "$F$: ratio of between cluster variance ",
                                "from a linear regression of each variable on cluster ",
                                "(imputed data: mean over the imputed data sets). ",
                                "\\% missing: percentage of participants with missing values."),
               label = "tab:desc")

# Two-level header built from computed quantities (no hand-written n)
head_top <- paste0("& & \\multicolumn{", kfin + 1, "}{c}{\\bf Raw data} & ",
                   "\\multicolumn{", kfin + 1, "}{c}{\\bf Imputed data} \\\\\n")
head_sub <- paste0("\\bf Variable & \\bf \\% missing & ",
                   paste0("\\bf Cl.~", 1:kfin, " ($n=", n_raw, "$)", collapse = " & "),
                   " & \\bf $F$ & ",
                   paste0("\\bf Cl.~", 1:kfin, " ($n=", n_imp_med, "$)", collapse = " & "),
                   " & \\bf $F$ \\\\\n")

print(desc,
      table.placement = "H",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = FALSE,
      include.colnames = FALSE,           # header is written by add.to.row
      add.to.row = list(pos = list(0),
                        command = paste0(head_top,
                                         "\\cmidrule(lr){3-", kfin + 3, "} ",
                                         "\\cmidrule(lr){", kfin + 4, "-", 2 * kfin + 4, "}\n",
                                         head_sub, "\n")),
      floating = TRUE,
      sanitize.text.function = force,
      booktabs = TRUE)
@
<<fig_barplot, fig.pos='H', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=6, out.width='0.9\\textwidth', fig.cap=paste0("\\label{fig:barplot}Percentage of the ", length(miclust_1$usedimp), " imputed data sets in which each variable was selected by the backward algorithm for $k=", miclust_1$kfin, "$, in decreasing order.")>>=

# Frequency of selection of each variable across the imputed data sets
y   <- getvariablesfrequency(miclust_1)

# Sort the variables by decreasing percentage of selection
y_ord <- order(y$percfreq, decreasing = TRUE)
perc <- y$percfreq[y_ord]
name <- y$varnames[y_ord]

# Bars drawn as thick vertical lines (type = "h", lwd = 20).
plot(perc, type = "h", main = "", xlab = "", ylab = "Percentage of times selected", 
     xlim = 0.5 + c(0, length(perc)), ylim = c(0, 100),
     lwd = 20, col = "skyblue", xaxt = "n")

# Variable names below each bar, las = 2 draws them vertically, and
# cex.axis = 0.9 shrinks them slightly so that the long names fit
axis(1, at = 1:length(name), labels = name, las = 2, cex.axis = 0.9)

# This code is adapted from the miclust README, which can be accessed at
# https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/miclust/readme/README.html
@

\section{Report}

Write a brief report (400 words maximum) describing the following issues, and
citing tables and figures:

\begin{itemize}
    \item The final number of clusters and their sizes and uncertainty (cite your
    figure(s) and table(s)).

    \item The agreement when relabeling the clusters.

    \item The final subset of clustering variables and uncertainty.

    \item The probability of assignment to each cluster and uncertainty.
\end{itemize}

<<report_values, include = FALSE>>=
var_once <- sum(perc>0) #selected at least once
var_cand <- ncol(copd_raw) #cadidate variables
var_sel <- length(summary_k$selectedvariables) #final selected variables
var_sel_all <- sum(perc == M) #selected variables in all data sets
p_last <- sprintf("%.0f", perc[var_sel]) #least frequent selected variable
p_next <- sprintf("%.0f", perc[var_sel + 1]) #next variable (not selected)
med1 <- paste(sprintf("%.2f", a1[, 3]), collapse = " and ")
min1 <- paste(sprintf("%.2f", a1[, 1]), collapse = " and ")
n_final <- table(factor(summary_k$cluster, levels = 1:kfin))
kappa_vals <- summary_k$kappas
kappa_med  <- sprintf("%.2f", median(kappa_vals))

@

To find the number of clusters, we applied the backward search with the CritCF criterion to each of the \Sexpr{M} imputed data sets. Figure~\ref{fig:fig_critcf} shows that even the worst value for $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$ is above the best value for $k = \Sexpr{kfin + 1}$, so $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$ was the optimal number of clusters in all the imputed data sets. Therefore, there is practically no uncertainty in the choice of $k$.
After the assignation, the clusters contain \Sexpr{n_final[1]} and \Sexpr{n_final[2]} subjects.

To prevent a potential problem caused by the automatic labeling of the clusters, the clusters were relabeled taking the first data set as a reference and evaluating them with Cohen's kappa, same as the article. The agreement after relabeling is good, with a median Cohen's kappa of \Sexpr{kappa_med}.

For $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$, \Sexpr{var_once} of \Sexpr{var_cand} variables candidates were selected in at least one data set(Figure~\ref{fig:fig_varsel} and \ref{fig:fig_barplot}).
The final number of selected variables is \Sexpr{var_sel}, with \Sexpr{var_sel_all} of those that are in all the imputed data sets.
There is a clear drop in frequency right after the last selected variable (from \Sexpr{p_last}\% to \Sexpr{p_next}\%). Also, we see that the selected variables would be the same if we kept those appearing in more than 50\% of the data sets. Therefore, the variable selection is very robust to the missing data.

Each subject was assigned to the cluster to which they were allocated most often across the imputed data sets.  In table ~\ref{tab:alloc} we can see how this frequency is distributed in  $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$ and  $k = \Sexpr{kfin + 1}$.  For $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$, the median probability is \Sexpr{med1} in both clusters, so most subjects are always assigned to the same cluster.  For $k = \Sexpr{kfin + 1}$, the assignment is also very stable, and this is probably because the selected variables have few missing data, so the imputed data sets are very similar. Given this, we see that a stable assignment does not necessarily mean that the clusters are adequate, we have already seen that the choice of $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$ is supported by the CritCF criterion.
Table~\ref{tab:desc} shows that cluster 2 groups the subjects with more severe disease with worse lung function, more air trapping, lower arterial oxygen and worse scores in the questionnaires. We also see that results with raw and imputed data are very similar.


